1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,725
At twenty-seven minutes and fifty-three seconds
past one in the morning,

2
00:00:03,725 --> 00:00:06,827
a duty officer in Baltimore gave an order to stop
the traffic.

3
00:00:07,127 --> 00:00:10,025
Sixty-seven seconds later the bridge
was in the river,

4
00:00:10,025 --> 00:00:12,932
and six men who had been filling potholes on it
were gone.

5
00:00:13,232 --> 00:00:17,897
Everyone who could act, acted. So how
did the sixty-seven seconds run out?

6
00:00:19,697 --> 00:00:23,830
There is a particular kind of American job
that only exists after midnight.

7
00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:26,569
The road belongs to everybody during the day,

8
00:00:26,569 --> 00:00:30,374
so the work on it has to happen when nobody wants
it. Cones.

9
00:00:30,674 --> 00:00:32,299
A truck with a light bar.

10
00:00:32,599 --> 00:00:36,165
A crew that knows each other's coffee order
and works in the cold

11
00:00:36,165 --> 00:00:37,847
with the river underneath them.

12
00:00:38,147 --> 00:00:41,081
On the night of the twenty-fifth of March, 2024,

13
00:00:41,081 --> 00:00:45,717
that crew was on the Francis Scott Key Bridge
in Baltimore, patching the deck.

14
00:00:46,017 --> 00:00:50,707
Eight people were up there — a seven-person road
maintenance crew and one inspector.

15
00:00:51,007 --> 00:00:53,497
Below them, at the Seagirt Marine Terminal,

16
00:00:53,497 --> 00:00:57,669
a container ship was finishing her cargo. She
was called the Dali.

17
00:00:57,969 --> 00:01:04,192
She flew the flag of Singapore,
and her international number was 9697428.

18
00:01:04,492 --> 00:01:07,915
She had come to the United States
on the nineteenth of March,

19
00:01:07,915 --> 00:01:10,684
worked Newark for two days, then Norfolk for one,

20
00:01:10,684 --> 00:01:15,255
and moored in Baltimore Harbor on the twenty-third
at thirty-six minutes past two

21
00:01:15,255 --> 00:01:16,102
in the morning.

22
00:01:16,402 --> 00:01:21,371
She was a working ship on a working schedule,
and she was bound for Sri Lanka.

23
00:01:21,671 --> 00:01:22,739
She was loaded.

24
00:01:23,039 --> 00:01:27,433
Not riding high and empty on her way to pick
something up — loaded, which matters,

25
00:01:27,433 --> 00:01:30,384
because a loaded ship carries her momentum
differently

26
00:01:30,384 --> 00:01:33,928
and answers a rudder differently. Two halves
of one picture.

27
00:01:34,228 --> 00:01:36,736
The night lives in the distance between them.

28
00:01:37,036 --> 00:01:39,776
Up on the deck, the work is small and physical.

29
00:01:40,076 --> 00:01:42,862
Somebody is walking backwards guiding a truck.

30
00:01:43,162 --> 00:01:46,273
Somebody is checking that a cone has not blown
over.

31
00:01:46,573 --> 00:01:50,712
It is the kind of job where the most dangerous
thing you expect is a car

32
00:01:50,712 --> 00:01:55,185
that does not slow down. Down at the terminal,
the work is enormous and invisible.

33
00:01:55,485 --> 00:01:59,200
A ship this size is a floating power station
with cargo strapped to it.

34
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:02,333
She makes her own electricity from four diesel
generators.

35
00:02:02,633 --> 00:02:04,909
She distributes it on a high-voltage bus.

36
00:02:05,209 --> 00:02:09,999
She steps it down through transformers to run
everything a person would recognise

37
00:02:09,999 --> 00:02:12,779
as a ship — the lights, the pumps, the steering.

38
00:02:13,079 --> 00:02:17,047
Neither group can see the other's risks.
That is not a criticism.

39
00:02:17,347 --> 00:02:19,344
That is just what a port is.

40
00:02:19,644 --> 00:02:23,870
And there was one more witness aboard that neither
of them would have thought about.

41
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:28,253
International rules require a ship of the Dali's
tonnage and age

42
00:02:28,253 --> 00:02:32,761
to carry a voyage data recorder — the maritime
equivalent of a black box.

43
00:02:33,061 --> 00:02:36,435
Through everything that followed, with the ship's
lighting gone twice,

44
00:02:36,435 --> 00:02:40,141
the bridge audio kept recording. Somebody
would hear this night.

45
00:02:40,441 --> 00:02:41,837
At about a quarter to one,

46
00:02:41,837 --> 00:02:45,085
the senior pilot ordered the main engine to dead
slow ahead.

47
00:02:45,385 --> 00:02:47,521
That engine was not a small thing.

48
00:02:47,821 --> 00:02:52,233
Fifty-five thousand six hundred and twenty-six
horsepower, turning one propeller.

49
00:02:52,533 --> 00:02:58,914
Call it five hundred family cars. One shaft. One
loaded ship. In the dark.

50
00:02:59,214 --> 00:03:03,719
At about seven minutes past one, once she
was in the channel, the tugboats let go.

51
00:03:04,019 --> 00:03:07,823
That is normal. Every night. Every port.

52
00:03:08,123 --> 00:03:12,370
From that moment she moved under her own power.
Two pilots aboard.

53
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:14,899
A bridge she had passed under before.

54
00:03:15,199 --> 00:03:19,471
At about twenty-five minutes past one, she was six
tenths of a mile out.

55
00:03:19,771 --> 00:03:22,858
Three of her own lengths. And the lights went out.

56
00:03:23,158 --> 00:03:27,802
Two electrical breakers — HR1 and LR1 — opened
without being told to.

57
00:03:28,102 --> 00:03:32,467
In the space of that, the ship lost her lighting
and most of her equipment.

58
00:03:32,767 --> 00:03:35,414
She lost the pumps that cooled the main engine.

59
00:03:35,714 --> 00:03:38,036
She lost the pumps that moved the steering gear.

60
00:03:38,336 --> 00:03:41,773
The emergency generator started and picked
up the emergency bus.

61
00:03:42,073 --> 00:03:43,373
And the crew went to work.

62
00:03:43,673 --> 00:03:48,271
They closed HR1 and LR1 by hand, and the power
came back to the whole vessel.

63
00:03:48,571 --> 00:03:52,983
At twenty-six minutes and thirty-nine seconds past
one, the pilots called for a tug.

64
00:03:53,283 --> 00:03:56,209
The Eric McAllister answered immediately
and turned toward them.

65
00:03:56,509 --> 00:03:59,782
She was three miles away. She never reached
the ship.

66
00:04:00,082 --> 00:04:02,931
The pilots' dispatcher got on the phone
to the Coast Guard

67
00:04:02,931 --> 00:04:04,726
and told them the Dali had lost power.

68
00:04:05,026 --> 00:04:08,416
Then, two tenths of a mile from the bridge, it
happened again.

69
00:04:08,716 --> 00:04:11,573
Two more breakers — DGR3 and DGR4 — opened,

70
00:04:11,573 --> 00:04:16,100
and this time the generators came
off the high-voltage bus entirely.

71
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:18,583
Total loss of power, high side and low.

72
00:04:18,883 --> 00:04:21,868
At twenty-seven minutes and twenty-five seconds
past one,

73
00:04:21,868 --> 00:04:25,060
a pilot picked up the radio and warned every
vessel on the water.

74
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:26,705
Seven seconds after that,

75
00:04:26,705 --> 00:04:29,865
the duty officer in Baltimore gave his order
about the traffic.

76
00:04:30,165 --> 00:04:32,794
And thirty-one seconds after the second blackout,

77
00:04:32,794 --> 00:04:36,481
the crew closed two different breakers by hand
and got the lights back.

78
00:04:36,781 --> 00:04:39,846
They got the electricity back before the ship
touched the bridge.

79
00:04:40,146 --> 00:04:43,929
They did not get the propeller back. One more
thing was true.

80
00:04:44,229 --> 00:04:47,015
The emergency power could run a single steering
pump.

81
00:04:47,315 --> 00:04:50,064
But that pump, running alone on emergency power,

82
00:04:50,064 --> 00:04:54,560
was designed to turn the rudder more slowly
than it would with all pumps working.

83
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:58,007
And a rudder needs water moving past it to steer
at all —

84
00:04:58,007 --> 00:05:02,290
so without the propeller turning, it
would have been less effective anyway.

85
00:05:02,590 --> 00:05:05,955
The ship had a wheel. Turning it did almost
nothing.

86
00:05:06,255 --> 00:05:11,085
At about twenty-nine minutes past one, the Dali
struck pier number seventeen.

87
00:05:11,385 --> 00:05:12,964
The piers were not naked.

88
00:05:13,264 --> 00:05:17,859
They were protected by structures called dolphins
— sheet pile and concrete,

89
00:05:17,859 --> 00:05:20,927
built to take a hit and keep a ship
off the column.

90
00:05:21,227 --> 00:05:24,023
Two of them are drawn on the Safety Board's own
map

91
00:05:24,023 --> 00:05:27,286
of the Dali's last three minutes. They
were not enough.

92
00:05:27,586 --> 00:05:31,719
Why they were not enough is still standing
over other people's roads tonight.

93
00:05:32,019 --> 00:05:36,799
The span came down. Eight people had been on it.
Six of them died.

94
00:05:37,099 --> 00:05:40,118
Which raises the first thing that does not fit.

95
00:05:40,418 --> 00:05:42,946
If a ship losing power is a known event,

96
00:05:42,946 --> 00:05:46,542
and this bridge had protection built for exactly
that,

97
00:05:46,542 --> 00:05:52,295
why did the protection not hold? Everybody gets
this part wrong.

98
00:05:52,595 --> 00:05:55,799
The obvious answer is that a ship lost power
and hit a bridge.

99
00:05:56,099 --> 00:06:01,600
That is true, and it explains nothing,
because ships lose power. It is not rare.

100
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:05,183
A vessel is a small industrial city with four
generators

101
00:06:05,183 --> 00:06:09,491
and several thousand connections. Industrial
cities have bad nights.

102
00:06:09,791 --> 00:06:12,995
So the question was never whether the Dali
could lose power.

103
00:06:13,295 --> 00:06:16,453
A ship of this class is built on the assumption
that she will.

104
00:06:16,753 --> 00:06:19,911
The whole architecture exists to make a blackout
survivable.

105
00:06:20,211 --> 00:06:22,740
Two step-down transformers, TR1 and TR2,

106
00:06:22,740 --> 00:06:25,505
either of which can feed the low-voltage side.

107
00:06:25,805 --> 00:06:28,452
A breaker on each side of each transformer.

108
00:06:28,752 --> 00:06:31,929
A bus tie that can be closed to run the two halves
as one,

109
00:06:31,929 --> 00:06:34,093
which is how she was running that night.

110
00:06:34,393 --> 00:06:35,292
And critically:

111
00:06:35,292 --> 00:06:39,793
the ship's critical systems were originally
designed with reliable redundancies

112
00:06:39,793 --> 00:06:41,872
and automatic restart capabilities,

113
00:06:41,872 --> 00:06:45,213
so that after a blackout she could get her power
back quickly.

114
00:06:45,513 --> 00:06:50,921
That is not an opinion. That is what the United
States told a grand jury this year.

115
00:06:51,221 --> 00:06:54,797
Now look at what the humans on the water actually
did in those four minutes.

116
00:06:55,097 --> 00:06:59,970
The engineers found the breakers and closed them
by hand — twice, under a blackout,

117
00:06:59,970 --> 00:07:01,320
at night, in a channel.

118
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:05,707
The pilots called for a tug within seventy-four
seconds of the first blackout.

119
00:07:06,007 --> 00:07:08,515
The pilots' dispatcher called the Coast Guard.

120
00:07:08,815 --> 00:07:12,855
A pilot broadcast a warning to everything floating
in the Patapsco.

121
00:07:13,155 --> 00:07:17,356
The duty officer stopped the traffic. The tug
turned around and ran.

122
00:07:17,656 --> 00:07:22,216
Every one of those is a person doing exactly
the thing their training says to do,

123
00:07:22,216 --> 00:07:25,319
in the right order, faster than most of us
would manage.

124
00:07:25,619 --> 00:07:29,728
The tug was three miles out. The bridge was two
tenths of a mile ahead.

125
00:07:30,028 --> 00:07:32,025
Nobody was going to close that gap.

126
00:07:32,325 --> 00:07:35,529
And the second wrong answer usually arrives right
here.

127
00:07:35,829 --> 00:07:38,061
If the tug could not get there in time,

128
00:07:38,061 --> 00:07:43,374
then surely the mistake was letting the tugs go
in the first place. It was not.

129
00:07:43,674 --> 00:07:45,253
That is the procedure.

130
00:07:45,553 --> 00:07:49,140
Tugs escort a ship out of the berth
and through the tight water,

131
00:07:49,140 --> 00:07:52,673
and once she is in the channel and making way
under her own power,

132
00:07:52,673 --> 00:07:53,726
they are released.

133
00:07:54,026 --> 00:07:58,173
About seven minutes past one, in the channel,
that is what happened,

134
00:07:58,173 --> 00:08:01,224
and the Safety Board records it as normal
practice.

135
00:08:01,524 --> 00:08:04,728
Every ship leaving Baltimore that week
did the same thing.

136
00:08:05,028 --> 00:08:08,511
Every ship leaving most ports in the world
does the same thing tonight.

137
00:08:08,811 --> 00:08:12,882
A rule that only looks wrong once, in hindsight,
after a wire fails,

138
00:08:12,882 --> 00:08:14,523
is not the thing that failed.

139
00:08:14,823 --> 00:08:18,678
So walk the four minutes again and ask what lever
was actually available.

140
00:08:18,978 --> 00:08:22,470
The emergency generator started and picked
up the emergency bus,

141
00:08:22,470 --> 00:08:27,472
exactly as designed. The engineers found
and closed the breakers by hand. Twice.

142
00:08:27,772 --> 00:08:32,902
In the dark. The pilots called for help, warned
the water, and told the Coast Guard.

143
00:08:33,202 --> 00:08:34,688
The shore stopped the cars.

144
00:08:34,988 --> 00:08:38,657
And every one of those levers moves something
other than the ship.

145
00:08:38,957 --> 00:08:43,193
Because the one thing that would have changed
the outcome was the propeller,

146
00:08:43,193 --> 00:08:47,781
and the propeller was downstream of the exact
system that kept switching itself off.

147
00:08:48,081 --> 00:08:51,772
The main engine was a separate machine
from the four generators —

148
00:08:51,772 --> 00:08:55,566
but the pumps that cooled it, and the pumps
that drove the steering,

149
00:08:55,566 --> 00:08:59,898
lived on the low-voltage bus that went dark.
That is the shape of the trap.

150
00:09:00,198 --> 00:09:04,631
The ship was not disabled because her engine
broke. Her engine did not break.

151
00:09:04,931 --> 00:09:05,878
She was disabled

152
00:09:05,878 --> 00:09:10,137
because the small electrical support around a very
large engine went away,

153
00:09:10,137 --> 00:09:12,360
and a very large engine without cooling

154
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:15,798
and without steering assistance
is not a propulsion system.

155
00:09:16,098 --> 00:09:18,281
It is ballast with a crankshaft in it.

156
00:09:18,581 --> 00:09:21,969
There is one more detail in the record that makes
this concrete,

157
00:09:21,969 --> 00:09:25,083
and it is the sort of thing only an investigator
would notice.

158
00:09:25,383 --> 00:09:28,262
The ship's data recorder never stopped listening.

159
00:09:28,562 --> 00:09:31,956
International rules require a vessel
of her tonnage,

160
00:09:31,956 --> 00:09:36,627
built after the middle of 2014 and trading
internationally, to carry one —

161
00:09:36,627 --> 00:09:40,088
and through both blackouts, with most of the ship
dark,

162
00:09:40,088 --> 00:09:42,494
the bridge audio kept being captured.

163
00:09:42,794 --> 00:09:46,649
Which means we are not reconstructing
this from memory or from lawyers.

164
00:09:46,949 --> 00:09:48,842
There is a recording of four minutes

165
00:09:48,842 --> 00:09:52,393
in which a professional crew did the right things
in the right order

166
00:09:52,393 --> 00:09:53,543
and it did not matter.

167
00:09:53,843 --> 00:09:56,646
That is a rare and horrible kind of evidence,

168
00:09:56,646 --> 00:09:59,602
and it forecloses the comfortable explanation.

169
00:09:59,902 --> 00:10:03,626
There is no moment on that tape where somebody
freezes, or argues,

170
00:10:03,626 --> 00:10:05,846
or fails to understand what is happening.

171
00:10:06,146 --> 00:10:10,093
What there is, instead, is a ship that kept coming
back to life from the neck up.

172
00:10:10,393 --> 00:10:14,832
At twenty-seven minutes and thirty-two seconds
past one she had her lights,

173
00:10:14,832 --> 00:10:17,452
her low-voltage bus and generator number two.

174
00:10:17,752 --> 00:10:22,117
Ninety seconds earlier she had had all
of that too, and then lost it again.

175
00:10:22,417 --> 00:10:26,908
Twice in four minutes, the thing that was designed
never to happen twice in a row,

176
00:10:26,908 --> 00:10:31,355
happened twice in a row. So the story
is not a story about people failing.

177
00:10:31,655 --> 00:10:35,961
It is a story about a machine that was supposed
to come back and did not come back

178
00:10:35,961 --> 00:10:39,490
in time — and about the difference between getting
your lights back

179
00:10:39,490 --> 00:10:40,897
and getting your ship back.

180
00:10:41,197 --> 00:10:45,980
At twenty-seven minutes and thirty-two seconds
past one, the Dali had electricity.

181
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:51,409
She had lighting. She had her low-voltage bus, fed
from generator number two.

182
00:10:51,709 --> 00:10:54,214
What she did not have was a turning propeller,

183
00:10:54,214 --> 00:10:57,746
and without a turning propeller a rudder
is a piece of furniture.

184
00:10:58,046 --> 00:11:01,111
Water has to move across it for it to do anything
at all.

185
00:11:01,411 --> 00:11:02,921
She was, at that moment,

186
00:11:02,921 --> 00:11:07,308
nine hundred and forty-seven feet of loaded steel
with the lights on,

187
00:11:07,308 --> 00:11:10,513
going where the tide and her own momentum took
her.

188
00:11:10,813 --> 00:11:12,328
Which leaves one question,

189
00:11:12,328 --> 00:11:16,154
and it is the question two federal investigations
spent two years on.

190
00:11:16,454 --> 00:11:22,527
Why did the power keep going out?
Before the failures, the reasons.

191
00:11:22,827 --> 00:11:27,239
This is a system that was designed by people
who had thought about this exact night.

192
00:11:27,539 --> 00:11:31,298
A vessel like the Dali distributes power
on a high-voltage bus fed

193
00:11:31,298 --> 00:11:32,740
by four diesel generators.

194
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:37,634
A four-hundred-and-forty-volt low-voltage bus
hangs off it through two step-down

195
00:11:37,634 --> 00:11:42,702
transformers. The low-voltage side is where
the ordinary life of the ship lives:

196
00:11:42,702 --> 00:11:47,272
the lighting, the steering gear pumps, the main
engine cooling water pumps.

197
00:11:47,572 --> 00:11:53,027
The redundancy is deliberate and it is doubled.
Two transformers, so one can fail.

198
00:11:53,327 --> 00:11:56,438
Breakers either side of each, so a fault
can be isolated.

199
00:11:56,738 --> 00:11:58,905
Generators that restart on their own,

200
00:11:58,905 --> 00:12:03,725
so a blackout does not need a human in the loop
to end. That is the design.

201
00:12:04,025 --> 00:12:05,093
Now the night.

202
00:12:05,393 --> 00:12:09,440
Ten hours before the Dali sailed, while she
was still tied up in Baltimore,

203
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:11,570
the lights went out on her once already.

204
00:12:11,870 --> 00:12:14,625
A crewmember was working on the exhaust scrubber
system

205
00:12:14,625 --> 00:12:17,443
for the engine driving the only generator
that was online.

206
00:12:17,743 --> 00:12:22,362
He closed an inline exhaust damper by mistake.
The exhaust had nowhere to go.

207
00:12:22,662 --> 00:12:25,681
The generator stopped, and the ship went dark
at the pier.

208
00:12:25,981 --> 00:12:29,814
Then it happened a second time in port,
for a different reason —

209
00:12:29,814 --> 00:12:34,919
not enough fuel pressure for the online generator.
Nobody died in either of those.

210
00:12:35,219 --> 00:12:39,538
They are the kind of thing that gets written
in a log and talked about over a meal.

211
00:12:39,838 --> 00:12:43,773
So why would a night at a pier where nothing
happened turn out to matter more

212
00:12:43,773 --> 00:12:45,457
than anything else in this story?

213
00:12:45,757 --> 00:12:48,915
Because of what was done to put the ship right
afterwards.

214
00:12:49,215 --> 00:12:53,346
Something happened during the recovery
from the second in-port blackout,

215
00:12:53,346 --> 00:12:55,484
and it is the hinge of the entire night.

216
00:12:55,784 --> 00:12:59,081
Coming back up, the crew changed the ship's
electrical configuration.

217
00:12:59,381 --> 00:13:04,025
They stopped using breakers HR2 and LR2
with transformer TR2 —

218
00:13:04,025 --> 00:13:09,505
which had been in use for several months —
and switched to HR1, LR1 and TR1.

219
00:13:09,805 --> 00:13:13,056
And that is the configuration the Dali sailed
on the next morning.

220
00:13:13,356 --> 00:13:17,285
Ten hours later, six tenths of a mile from the Key
Bridge,

221
00:13:17,285 --> 00:13:19,254
it was HR1 and LR1 that opened.

222
00:13:19,554 --> 00:13:23,176
It took the National Transportation Safety Board
twenty months to say why.

223
00:13:23,476 --> 00:13:25,724
On the eighteenth of November, 2025,

224
00:13:25,724 --> 00:13:30,278
the Board met and determined the probable cause.
It was a wire.

225
00:13:30,578 --> 00:13:34,343
Specifically: a loose signal wire connection
to a terminal block,

226
00:13:34,343 --> 00:13:37,981
stemming from the improper installation
of wire-label banding,

227
00:13:37,981 --> 00:13:40,363
which caused the loss of electrical power,

228
00:13:40,363 --> 00:13:44,324
which caused the loss of propulsion and steering
close to the bridge.

229
00:13:44,624 --> 00:13:47,735
The mechanism is small enough to hold between two
fingers.

230
00:13:48,035 --> 00:13:51,379
A terminal block has a spring-clamp gate
that grips a wire.

231
00:13:51,679 --> 00:13:54,373
Somebody had put a band of labelling around
that wire.

232
00:13:54,673 --> 00:13:57,413
The band stopped the wire seating fully
into the gate.

233
00:13:57,713 --> 00:14:01,031
The connection was inadequate. That is the whole
thing.

234
00:14:01,331 --> 00:14:04,757
A ship of this size, a bridge of this size, six
men,

235
00:14:04,757 --> 00:14:09,980
and the physical cause is a label
that was a fraction of a millimetre too thick

236
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:13,519
in the wrong place. But that explains the first
blackout.

237
00:14:13,819 --> 00:14:16,559
It does not explain why she did not come back.

238
00:14:16,859 --> 00:14:20,920
Because remember: she was built to come back.
Automatic restart.

239
00:14:21,220 --> 00:14:25,096
Reliable redundancies. That was the promise
in the drawings.

240
00:14:25,396 --> 00:14:30,609
On the twelfth of May, 2026, a federal court
in Maryland unsealed an indictment,

241
00:14:30,609 --> 00:14:35,381
and the United States offered its account of why
the promise was not kept.

242
00:14:35,681 --> 00:14:39,061
Before a word of it: an indictment is merely
an accusation,

243
00:14:39,061 --> 00:14:41,459
and all defendants are presumed innocent

244
00:14:41,459 --> 00:14:45,062
until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt
in a court of law.

245
00:14:45,362 --> 00:14:48,075
Those are the Department of Justice's own words,

246
00:14:48,075 --> 00:14:52,675
printed underneath its own press release.
This programme will hold to them.

247
00:14:52,975 --> 00:14:58,720
The three defendants are Synergy Marine,
of Singapore; Synergy Maritime, of Chennai,

248
00:14:58,720 --> 00:15:02,970
India; and Radhakrishnan Karthik Nair, forty-seven
years old,

249
00:15:02,970 --> 00:15:08,446
an Indian national who worked for both companies
as the technical superintendent

250
00:15:08,446 --> 00:15:09,322
for the Dali.

251
00:15:09,622 --> 00:15:13,089
They are charged with conspiracy to defraud
the United States,

252
00:15:13,089 --> 00:15:16,500
with willfully failing to immediately inform
the Coast Guard

253
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:20,591
of a known hazardous condition, with obstruction
of an agency proceeding,

254
00:15:20,591 --> 00:15:22,068
and with false statements.

255
00:15:22,368 --> 00:15:27,473
The two companies additionally face misdemeanour
environmental counts for what went

256
00:15:27,473 --> 00:15:31,470
into the Patapsco River — containers, oil,
and the bridge itself.

257
00:15:31,770 --> 00:15:34,046
The allegation about the machinery is this.

258
00:15:34,346 --> 00:15:38,405
The government says the Dali lost power twice
in a four-minute span,

259
00:15:38,405 --> 00:15:43,077
and that a loose wire in a high-voltage
switchboard likely caused the first loss.

260
00:15:43,377 --> 00:15:45,174
And then, the government says,

261
00:15:45,174 --> 00:15:48,607
the ship did not recover the way she was designed
to recover —

262
00:15:48,607 --> 00:15:50,854
because the defendants had altered her.

263
00:15:51,154 --> 00:15:52,860
According to the indictment,

264
00:15:52,860 --> 00:15:57,377
they relied on a flushing pump to supply fuel
to two of the Dali's four generators.

265
00:15:57,677 --> 00:16:00,185
A flushing pump is a housekeeping device.

266
00:16:00,485 --> 00:16:04,340
It is for moving fuel around, not for feeding
an engine in anger.

267
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:07,882
And the crucial property, in the government's
telling,

268
00:16:07,882 --> 00:16:11,838
is that it was not designed to automatically
restart after a blackout.

269
00:16:12,138 --> 00:16:17,082
So the chain the indictment draws is: the lights
go out, the generators need fuel,

270
00:16:17,082 --> 00:16:20,238
the pump that was feeding them cannot start
itself,

271
00:16:20,238 --> 00:16:22,448
and the ship blacks out a second time.

272
00:16:22,748 --> 00:16:26,510
And then comes the sentence that will be fought
over in court for years.

273
00:16:26,810 --> 00:16:31,120
The indictment alleges that if the Dali had used
the proper fuel supply pumps,

274
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:33,546
the vessel would have regained power in time

275
00:16:33,546 --> 00:16:39,785
to navigate safely under the Key Bridge. Alleges.
Not found. Not proven.

276
00:16:40,085 --> 00:16:44,810
A grand jury heard a case and returned a charge,
and that is a beginning,

277
00:16:44,810 --> 00:16:45,658
not an ending.

278
00:16:45,958 --> 00:16:50,648
And then, five weeks later, something happened
that is not an allegation at all.

279
00:16:50,948 --> 00:16:52,870
On the eighteenth of June, 2026,

280
00:16:52,870 --> 00:16:57,343
the chief engineer of the Dali entered
into a deferred prosecution agreement

281
00:16:57,343 --> 00:17:01,418
with the United States. His name is Karthikeyan
Deenadayalan.

282
00:17:01,718 --> 00:17:03,158
He was aboard that night.

283
00:17:03,458 --> 00:17:07,263
In that agreement he admitted to failing to report
a hazardous condition,

284
00:17:07,263 --> 00:17:09,867
in violation of the Ports and Waterways Safety
Act.

285
00:17:10,167 --> 00:17:12,721
And the things he acknowledged are worth hearing
slowly.

286
00:17:13,021 --> 00:17:17,313
He had also been chief engineer on two
of the Dali's sister ships —

287
00:17:17,313 --> 00:17:19,569
the Maersk Saltoro and the Cezanne.

288
00:17:19,869 --> 00:17:24,906
And he knew that all three of them used an unsafe
fuel supply pump. Not one ship.

289
00:17:25,206 --> 00:17:25,949
Three.

290
00:17:26,249 --> 00:17:28,846
He acknowledged that the pump lacked redundancy,

291
00:17:28,846 --> 00:17:33,301
and that this compromised the vessels' safe
navigation and their ability to recover

292
00:17:33,301 --> 00:17:34,376
from a loss of power.

293
00:17:34,676 --> 00:17:37,732
And he knew that a ship unable to recover
from a loss

294
00:17:37,732 --> 00:17:42,694
of power could adversely affect the safety
of the vessel itself — and of any bridge,

295
00:17:42,694 --> 00:17:47,700
structure or shore area. That sentence was written
in a legal agreement in 2026.

296
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:53,062
It describes, in the abstract, exactly what
happened in the Patapsco in 2024.

297
00:17:53,362 --> 00:17:57,886
He also said he had spoken and corresponded
with people at Synergy about using

298
00:17:57,886 --> 00:18:00,421
that pump, including the co-defendant, Nair.

299
00:18:00,721 --> 00:18:04,108
And he said something else, and here the guardrail
goes back up,

300
00:18:04,108 --> 00:18:07,215
because this is one man's account of another man's
conduct

301
00:18:07,215 --> 00:18:09,173
and that other man has not been tried.

302
00:18:09,473 --> 00:18:13,355
The chief engineer said that Nair directed him
to send a convincing email

303
00:18:13,355 --> 00:18:14,664
to the ship's charterer,

304
00:18:14,664 --> 00:18:18,508
so that the charterer would not ask more questions
about fuel consumption

305
00:18:18,508 --> 00:18:21,129
and find out that the flushing pump
was being used.

306
00:18:21,429 --> 00:18:22,716
There is one more count,

307
00:18:22,716 --> 00:18:25,701
and it is the one that tells you what kind
of story this is.

308
00:18:26,001 --> 00:18:28,918
The obstruction charge relates, among other
things,

309
00:18:28,918 --> 00:18:31,920
to what Nair is alleged to have told
the investigators:

310
00:18:31,920 --> 00:18:36,171
that he was unaware the Dali was using
the flushing pump to fuel the generators.

311
00:18:36,471 --> 00:18:41,158
An alteration to a ship. In an engine room. Below
the waterline.

312
00:18:41,458 --> 00:18:45,591
Which, the United States says, the investigators
had to find rather than be told.

313
00:18:45,891 --> 00:18:47,795
And what about the bridge in all this?

314
00:18:48,095 --> 00:18:53,210
The Board looked at that too, and its finding
is quieter and, for anyone who drives,

315
00:18:53,210 --> 00:18:57,346
worse. Contributing to the collapse
and to the loss of life was the absence

316
00:18:57,346 --> 00:19:00,983
of countermeasures to reduce the bridge's
vulnerability to being struck

317
00:19:00,983 --> 00:19:02,297
by an ocean-going vessel —

318
00:19:02,297 --> 00:19:04,816
countermeasures that could have been put in place

319
00:19:04,816 --> 00:19:09,014
if the Maryland Transportation Authority
had carried out a vulnerability assessment.

320
00:19:09,314 --> 00:19:12,658
The guidance recommending those assessments
is longstanding.

321
00:19:12,958 --> 00:19:15,804
It comes from the American Association of State
Highway

322
00:19:15,804 --> 00:19:19,506
and Transportation Officials, and it
has been sitting there for years.

323
00:19:19,806 --> 00:19:23,382
And here the Board did something careful
that is worth noticing.

324
00:19:23,682 --> 00:19:26,097
It did not find that Maryland knew and ignored it.

325
00:19:26,397 --> 00:19:31,156
It found that they were likely unaware
of the potential risk. Not a cover-up.

326
00:19:31,456 --> 00:19:35,914
Something more ordinary and harder to fix:
a recommendation that existed,

327
00:19:35,914 --> 00:19:39,258
and a bridge whose owner did not know it applied
to them.

328
00:19:39,558 --> 00:19:43,991
A vulnerability assessment sounds like a filing
cabinet. It is a sum.

329
00:19:44,291 --> 00:19:45,731
Take the bridge you have.

330
00:19:46,031 --> 00:19:48,154
Take the ships that use the channel now,

331
00:19:48,154 --> 00:19:50,861
not the ships that used it when the bridge
was drawn.

332
00:19:51,161 --> 00:19:55,526
Work out what happens to that pier if that ship
arrives at that speed at that angle.

333
00:19:55,826 --> 00:20:00,491
Then find out whether the protection you already
have is protection. Or decoration.

334
00:20:00,791 --> 00:20:03,252
The dolphins by pier seventeen were real.

335
00:20:03,552 --> 00:20:07,128
Sheet pile and concrete, built by people
who were not careless.

336
00:20:07,428 --> 00:20:09,704
But protection is never absolute.

337
00:20:10,004 --> 00:20:14,230
It is protection against something, at some speed,
of some size.

338
00:20:14,530 --> 00:20:18,805
And a bridge stays exactly where it
is while the ships underneath it keep getting

339
00:20:18,805 --> 00:20:25,229
bigger. That is the failure. Not neglect.
Arithmetic that went quietly out of date.

340
00:20:25,529 --> 00:20:29,457
So how many other bridges are standing
over shipping channels tonight

341
00:20:29,457 --> 00:20:31,520
without anyone having done that sum?

342
00:20:33,320 --> 00:20:38,264
The Board answered that question on the twentieth
of March, 2025. Sixty-eight.

343
00:20:38,564 --> 00:20:41,005
Sixty-eight bridges in the United States

344
00:20:41,005 --> 00:20:44,369
for which the NTSB recommended a vulnerability
evaluation.

345
00:20:44,669 --> 00:20:47,270
Not sixty-eight bridges that are unsafe.

346
00:20:47,570 --> 00:20:50,855
Sixty-eight bridges where nobody had done
the arithmetic —

347
00:20:50,855 --> 00:20:54,960
where the owner could not say what would happen
if a modern ship hit a pier,

348
00:20:54,960 --> 00:20:58,065
because the question had never been asked
in that form.

349
00:20:58,365 --> 00:21:00,501
Every one of those is somebody's commute.

350
00:21:00,801 --> 00:21:04,888
Meanwhile, the money moved through a different
courthouse, and it moved fast.

351
00:21:05,188 --> 00:21:09,088
Six days after the collapse, on the first
of April, 2024,

352
00:21:09,088 --> 00:21:11,145
the ship's owner and manager —

353
00:21:11,145 --> 00:21:15,585
Grace Ocean Private Limited and Synergy Marine
Private Limited —

354
00:21:15,585 --> 00:21:21,256
filed in the District of Maryland for exoneration
from, or limitation of, liability.

355
00:21:21,556 --> 00:21:24,326
That is a very old piece of American maritime law,

356
00:21:24,326 --> 00:21:28,104
and it does something that sounds impossible
when you first hear it.

357
00:21:28,404 --> 00:21:32,901
It allows the owner of a vessel to ask a court
to cap what they can be made to pay,

358
00:21:32,901 --> 00:21:39,060
at roughly the value of what is left of the ship.
Before the outrage, the reason.

359
00:21:39,360 --> 00:21:44,304
That law exists because of a hard problem. A ship
is an asset that leaves.

360
00:21:44,604 --> 00:21:48,417
She sails out of reach, under people her owner
cannot see,

361
00:21:48,417 --> 00:21:50,363
into weather nobody controls.

362
00:21:50,663 --> 00:21:55,734
If one voyage could destroy the company
that financed it, the argument runs,

363
00:21:55,734 --> 00:21:59,276
nobody finances ships. So the law strikes a deal.

364
00:21:59,576 --> 00:22:02,975
An owner who did not know about the fault,
and could not have known,

365
00:22:02,975 --> 00:22:06,821
may ask a court to cap what he owes at the value
of the vessel and her freight.

366
00:22:07,121 --> 00:22:10,372
In the abstract it is a sensible rule
for a trading nation.

367
00:22:10,672 --> 00:22:13,830
After a bridge falls on a work crew it
is a number.

368
00:22:14,130 --> 00:22:16,031
The figure they asked to be limited

369
00:22:16,031 --> 00:22:18,960
to was about forty-three point seven million
dollars.

370
00:22:19,260 --> 00:22:24,311
Against that, the United States put in a claim
of one hundred and three million,

371
00:22:24,311 --> 00:22:29,334
seventy-eight thousand and fifty-six dollars,
under the Rivers and Harbors Act,

372
00:22:29,334 --> 00:22:32,473
the Oil Pollution Act, and general maritime law —

373
00:22:32,473 --> 00:22:36,118
for what it had cost the government to answer
the collapse.

374
00:22:36,418 --> 00:22:39,481
The companies agreed to pay one hundred and one
million,

375
00:22:39,481 --> 00:22:44,241
nine hundred and eighty thousand dollars.
That is a settlement of a civil claim.

376
00:22:44,541 --> 00:22:48,516
It is not an admission of anything, and it
is not a criminal finding,

377
00:22:48,516 --> 00:22:51,879
and this programme is not going to let it stand
in for one.

378
00:22:52,179 --> 00:22:55,095
Then, in May of 2026, the criminal case arrived,

379
00:22:55,095 --> 00:22:58,773
and with it a number that resets the scale
of everything above.

380
00:22:59,073 --> 00:23:01,205
The indictment alleges the economic loss

381
00:23:01,205 --> 00:23:03,438
in this case is at least five billion dollars.

382
00:23:03,738 --> 00:23:08,336
Five billion, against a request to be capped
at forty-three point seven million.

383
00:23:08,636 --> 00:23:12,233
The gap between those two numbers is the entire
argument,

384
00:23:12,233 --> 00:23:16,471
and it will be argued by real lawyers in a real
courtroom in Maryland,

385
00:23:16,471 --> 00:23:18,249
and it is not finished today.

386
00:23:18,549 --> 00:23:23,292
The investigation that produced the charges ran
for two years and involved the FBI,

387
00:23:23,292 --> 00:23:25,529
the Coast Guard Investigative Service,

388
00:23:25,529 --> 00:23:29,635
and the criminal investigators
of the Environmental Protection Agency,

389
00:23:29,635 --> 00:23:33,178
some of whom were among the first people aboard
the wrecked ship.

390
00:23:33,478 --> 00:23:36,268
It is worth saying plainly where that leaves
things

391
00:23:36,268 --> 00:23:39,908
on the day this programme is made. The Safety
Board has finished.

392
00:23:40,208 --> 00:23:42,943
It met, it voted, it named a probable cause,

393
00:23:42,943 --> 00:23:45,734
and it said the complete report would follow.

394
00:23:46,034 --> 00:23:49,649
The civil claim by the United States is resolved,
by agreement,

395
00:23:49,649 --> 00:23:53,650
for a hundred and one million, nine hundred
and eighty thousand dollars.

396
00:23:53,950 --> 00:23:58,894
And the criminal case has barely begun.
An indictment was unsealed in May.

397
00:23:59,194 --> 00:24:02,863
Three defendants stand charged — two companies
and one man.

398
00:24:03,163 --> 00:24:07,410
None of them has been tried. None of them
has been convicted of anything.

399
00:24:07,710 --> 00:24:11,769
The presumption of innocence is not a formality
that a documentary gets to skip

400
00:24:11,769 --> 00:24:13,979
because the facts underneath are upsetting.

401
00:24:14,279 --> 00:24:17,916
There is also a distinction in that indictment
which is easy to miss

402
00:24:17,916 --> 00:24:19,759
and which will decide a great deal.

403
00:24:20,059 --> 00:24:23,403
The counts are not, in the main, about building
a bad ship.

404
00:24:23,703 --> 00:24:28,301
They are about what was done with a good one,
and about what was said afterwards.

405
00:24:28,601 --> 00:24:33,685
Conspiracy. Failing to tell the Coast Guard
about a known hazardous condition.

406
00:24:33,985 --> 00:24:37,628
Obstruction of an agency proceeding. False
statements.

407
00:24:37,928 --> 00:24:42,038
Read them in order and they describe a sequence:
something was changed,

408
00:24:42,038 --> 00:24:45,755
the change was not reported,
and when investigators came asking,

409
00:24:45,755 --> 00:24:47,959
the government says they were not told.

410
00:24:48,259 --> 00:24:52,667
Whether that sequence is true
is not for this programme to decide,

411
00:24:52,667 --> 00:24:54,250
and it is not decided yet.

412
00:24:54,550 --> 00:24:58,730
It is for twelve people in a courtroom in Maryland
who have not yet been chosen.

413
00:24:59,030 --> 00:25:03,128
And all of it — the Board's twenty months,
the grand jury's two years,

414
00:25:03,128 --> 00:25:05,692
the settlement, the sixty-eight bridges —

415
00:25:05,692 --> 00:25:09,990
all of it comes back to a stretch of time you
could hold your breath through.

416
00:25:10,290 --> 00:25:13,216
The duty officer's order went out at twenty-seven
minutes

417
00:25:13,216 --> 00:25:14,934
and fifty-three seconds past one.

418
00:25:15,234 --> 00:25:18,438
The ship touched the pier at about twenty-nine
minutes past one.

419
00:25:18,738 --> 00:25:20,131
Sixty-seven seconds.

420
00:25:20,431 --> 00:25:24,149
And in that window a decision made ten hours
earlier at a pier,

421
00:25:24,149 --> 00:25:28,330
and a modification made at some point
before that in an engine room,

422
00:25:28,330 --> 00:25:30,506
and a band of label on a single wire,

423
00:25:30,506 --> 00:25:33,295
all arrived at the same place at the same time.

424
00:25:33,595 --> 00:25:39,900
So what is the rule that was actually running
that night? Go back to the deck.

425
00:25:40,200 --> 00:25:44,308
The work light is still burning. The cone is still
standing in it.

426
00:25:44,608 --> 00:25:46,094
Nothing has happened yet.

427
00:25:46,394 --> 00:25:49,784
Down the channel, a ship is leaving a berth
with her lights on.

428
00:25:50,084 --> 00:25:54,563
Every lamp aboard her is lit. That is what
a working ship looks like.

429
00:25:55,163 --> 00:26:00,453
She loses them at twenty-five past one. She gets
them back. She loses them again.

430
00:26:00,753 --> 00:26:04,508
And at twenty-seven minutes and thirty-two seconds
past one,

431
00:26:04,508 --> 00:26:08,694
with two tenths of a mile to run, she gets them
back for the last time.

432
00:26:08,994 --> 00:26:14,470
Lit from bow to stern. Every lamp burning.
And not moving under her own power.

433
00:26:15,070 --> 00:26:18,853
The drawings promised she could recover. They
were honest drawings.

434
00:26:19,153 --> 00:26:23,648
Somewhere between those drawings and that water,
the United States says,

435
00:26:23,648 --> 00:26:27,605
the promise was edited — and the edit stayed
in the engine room.

436
00:26:27,905 --> 00:26:29,623
Nobody has been tried for that.

437
00:26:29,923 --> 00:26:33,983
On the other side of the channel, the guidance
about ship strikes had been written,

438
00:26:33,983 --> 00:26:37,261
and published, and had not reached the people
who owned the bridge.

439
00:26:37,561 --> 00:26:42,434
The Board found they were likely unaware.
Not a villain. A gap.

440
00:26:42,734 --> 00:26:45,932
Sixty-eight bridges are now on a list that says:

441
00:26:45,932 --> 00:26:48,818
go and check what happens if a ship hits this.

442
00:26:49,118 --> 00:26:52,973
That list did not exist on the twenty-fifth
of March, 2024.

443
00:26:53,573 --> 00:26:58,192
The work light on the deck goes off at dawn.
The cone gets stacked on the truck.

444
00:26:58,492 --> 00:27:02,136
The lane opens. Every light in this story came
back on.

445
00:27:02,436 --> 00:27:04,758
That was never the thing that had to hold.
